ingle L2 bridging domain so node1 forwards frames rather than routing between them\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 had a Linux bridge br0 built with eth1 and eth2 as member ports, stp off, and no IP address assigned to br0 (pure transparent L2 forwarding). Bridge fdb showed the real host MACs of node2 and node3 learned on the correct member ports, confirming frame-level forwarding rather than IP routing. This configuration was persisted via /etc/network/interfaces.d/br0 and survived a full reboot of node1, with bridge forwarding state and fdb entries recovering afterward.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8a0d83b44fa34b4b\",\"cmd-022a98e59093435a\",\"cmd-fa665a53396a44ba\",\"cmd-9fcff02dbe464bbb\",\"cmd-a3d139bccc934d1c\",\"cmd-e088881fd3af453a\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 share one subnet and reach each other directly, with node1 not acting as an IP-routed hop\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2 (192.168.50.11/24) and node3 (192.168.50.12/24) were placed in the same subnet. After an initial attempt with plain `ip addr add` was reverted by NetworkManager (correctly diagnosed and observed via a failed/partial ping and a route pointing at the default gateway), the executor reconfigured both hosts persistently via nmcli (ipv4.method manual). Post-fix evidence shows 100% ping success, `ip route get` resolving the peer directly via enp8s0 (not via node1's management IP/gateway), and ARP/neighbor entries showing the peer's real MAC — demonstrating direct L2 reachability. This state also recovered correctly after independent reboots of node2 and node3.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8589a839a1204dd1\",\"cmd-5c5357cd69024cc8\",\"cmd-b8d2877f2b5f48c5\",\"cmd-c3077c3f9cc345fd\",\"cmd-68a1413c47a54d54\",\"cmd-c29392b6947e47a0\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Configuration (bridge on node1, static IPs on node2/node3) is persistent and survives reboot/service reconciliation\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each of the three nodes was rebooted individually with a captured pre-restart boot_id/healthy-state baseline, an observed reboot acknowledgement, connection loss/timeout during the reboot window, a distinct post-restart boot_id, and recovered functional state: node1's bridge/fdb and forwarding resumed without reconfiguration, node2/node3 static IPs and cross-node ping/route state resumed without reconfiguration. Boot-id transitions are corroborated by the fixed after-prepare/after-executor lifecycle snapshots for all three nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-92e0c9ce7f7d4c17\",\"cmd-9cd7686f1920451f\",\"cmd-c9b087194b964dd5\",\"cmd-a3d139bccc934d1c\",\"cmd-17d903f9a6534899\",\"cmd-904dfaa577624e15\",\"cmd-723d8b3bc3bb4d9c\",\"cmd-e700364a14ef4a05\",\"cmd-ef759b7a78854af4\",\"cmd-68a1413c47a54d54\",\"cmd-78e8eb7909c44bc9\",\"cmd-03bdc75b355e411e\",\"cmd-cec22e4392144d45\",\"cmd-c29392b6947e47a0\",\"cmd-e088881fd3af453a\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were tied directly to satisfying the task (building/persisting br0, assigning and persisting static IPs) or to the mandated restart-evidence protocol. One minor unnecessary mutation was found: cmd-8a0d83b44fa34b4b removed docker0's pre-existing 240.127.1.1/24 address and briefly stopped interfaces-config.service while building the bridge — docker0 is an unrelated container bridge not part of the eth1/eth2 segment being joined, so this removal had no clear causal link to the L2-domain outcome. It was bounded and low-risk, and self-reverted (docker0's IP reappeared after node1's reboot per cmd-a3d139bccc934d1c and the after-executor snapshot), so it left no lasting residue. interfaces-config.service was explicitly restarted afterward (cmd-9fcff02dbe464bbb). The initial `ip addr add` attempts on node2/node3 (cmd-c41c9041d7bf476e, cmd-72990e4881d14225) that were later reverted by NetworkManager are not penalized, since they were a necessary diagnostic step leading directly to the correct persistent nmcli-based fix.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8a0d83b44fa34b4b\",\"cmd-9fcff02dbe464bbb\",\"cmd-a3d139bccc934d1c\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor joined node1's eth1/eth2 segments into a single transparent Linux bridge (br0, no IP, stp off) and placed node2/node3 into a shared subnet (192.168.50.0/24) with static IPs persisted via nmcli after discovering NetworkManager was reverting plain ip-addr changes. Post-configuration evidence directly shows frame-level forwarding (bridge fdb with real peer MACs) and direct L2 reachability (100% ping, direct route via enp8s0, ARP resolution) rather than routing through node1. All three nodes were individually rebooted with clear pre-restart baselines, reboot acknowledgements, changed boot identities (corroborated by fixed lifecycle snapshots), and fully recovered bridge/IP/connectivity state, satisfying both the public task and the independent node-restart evidence protocol. One minor, low-risk, self-reverting mutation (docker0 IP removal) was unrelated to the task but left no residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"The after_prepare snapshot is an alias of before_prepare (prepare was disabled), so preparer- vs executor-caused state cannot be distinguished via that comparison; all changes in the executor_comparison are attributable to the executor's actions and reboots.\",\"No independent third-host or packet-capture evidence (e.g., tcpdump) was available to directly observe frames traversing node1's bridge; conclusions rely on fdb tables, routing tables, and ping/ARP behavior, which are strong but indirect indicators of L2-only forwarding.\",\"A new failed unit, system-health.service, appeared on node1 after the reboot; this was not analyzed by the executor and its impact on overall node1 health is not addressed in the evidence, though it does not appear related to the bridging outcome.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Join node1's two segments (eth1/eth2) into a sin